course: dictionary
lesson: 10833
title: Venture: going somewhere new
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Venture is a word for going somewhere new, where you do not know what will happen. «We ventured into the cave.» The cave may be dark and risky, so the children were brave to go in. Look at the clues around the word: cave, dark and brave all point to danger.
explain: Venture can also mean to say something carefully when you are not sure it is right. «I will venture a guess: it is a fox.» The speaker does not know for certain, so they say it carefully. The words «venture a guess» tell you to be careful, not bold.
explain: In a business lesson, a venture is a new project that might make money or might lose it. «They put their savings into a new shop, a business venture.» The shop is new and risky, so it is a venture. Nothing ventured, nothing gained is a saying: you have to take a risk to get a good result.
explain: Old books use venture in other ways. In an old book, a character says, «never even ventured to call on him at his own home myself». Here ventured means dared, so the character never dared to visit. When an old word looks strange, try swapping in a word you know and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Venture means to go somewhere that may be unknown, risky or a bit dangerous.
fact: «We ventured into the cave.» means the group went into the cave, which might be dangerous.
fact: To venture a guess or opinion means to say it carefully, even though you are not sure.
fact: A venture as a noun is a new project or business with some risk.
fact: In old English, «venture to ask» means dare to ask.
exercise: «We ventured into the cave.» What does ventured mean here? | Slept in the cave | Painted the walls of the cave | Ran away from the cave | *Went into somewhere unknown and maybe risky
exercise: Mia says, «I'll venture a guess: it's a fox.» What does venture a guess mean? | Refuse to guess at all | *Say a guess carefully, though not sure | Shout a guess loudly, certain it is right | Write the answer in a book
exercise: «They put their savings into a new shop, a business venture.» What is a venture here? | A large, dusty box | A holiday trip abroad | *A new business that might make or lose money | A type of cave
test: What does the saying «nothing ventured, nothing gained» suggest? | Gaining things is easy if you stay still | Only lazy people win prizes | Always stay at home to be safe | *You have to take a risk to get a good result
test: In an old book, a character says, «never even ventured to call on him». What does ventured to call mean? | Forgot to ring the bell | *Dared to visit him | Phoned for a long time | Shouted at him
exam: Which sentence uses venture as a noun? | Sam ventures out every day | *They put their savings into a new shop, a business venture | Ventured a guess that the time was four | They ventured out into the rain
